The "buffer overflow detected" error during the `test_details` test case strongly suggests an issue with memory allocation or access, specifically writing beyond the bounds of an allocated buffer.

Upon reviewing the `tests/subsys/mgmt/mcumgr/enum_mgmt/src/main.c` file, the `struct details_entries` definition stands out:

```c
struct details_entries {
	char expected_name[4];
	uint8_t expected_handlers;
	bool expected_test;

	bool matched_name;
	bool matched_handlers;
	bool matched_test;
};
```

The `expected_name` field is declared as `char expected_name[4]`.
However, in the `test_details` and `test_details_extra` functions, `strcpy` is used to populate this field with strings like `OS_MGMT_NAME`, `ENUM_MGMT_NAME`, and `SHELL_MGMT_NAME`:

*   `OS_MGMT_NAME` is "os mgmt" (7 characters + null terminator = 8 bytes).
*   `ENUM_MGMT_NAME` is "enum mgmt" (9 characters + null terminator = 10 bytes).
*   `SHELL_MGMT_NAME` is "shell mgmt" (10 characters + null terminator = 11 bytes).

All these strings are longer than the 4-byte buffer allocated for `expected_name`. When `strcpy` attempts to copy these longer strings into `expected_name[4]`, it results in a buffer overflow, leading to the observed runtime error.

To fix this, the size of the `expected_name` array needs to be increased to accommodate the longest possible string, plus the null terminator. The longest string is "shell mgmt" (10 characters), so a size of at least 11 bytes is required. Increasing it to 16 bytes provides a safe margin and common alignment.

```
tests/subsys/mgmt/mcumgr/enum_mgmt/src/main.c
<<<<<<<< SEARCH
struct details_entries {
	char expected_name[4];
	uint8_t expected_handlers;
	bool expected_test;
========
struct details_entries {
	char expected_name[16];
	uint8_t expected_handlers;
	bool expected_test;
>>>>>>>> REPLACE
```
